<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="sesamin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5469" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; (+)-Sesamin belongs to the furofuran group of lignans and is widespread among vascular plants. |FRAME:TAX-4182| is broadly cultivated throughout the world because it contains high amounts of sesame oil in its seeds, a natural product which is highly valued because of its antioxidant properties. It has been found that these properties can be attributed to the furfuran lignans present in this oil such as piperitol, sesamin and sesamolin |CITS: [Lewis99]| |CITS: [DAVIN03]|. There are many biological effects reported that connect to (+)-sesamin and derivatives thereof which are beneficial for humans from the viewpoint of health care |CITS: [MacRae84]|. (+)-Sesamin was shown to provide protection against liver damage |CITS: [12617602]|, decreased lipid peroxidation |CITS: [14598914]| as a result of elevating the concentration of vitamin E |CITS: [11983822]|, and exhibited anti-inflammatory effects |CITS: [10966903]| and inhibition of vascular superoxide production |CITS: [14646174]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; All the lignans that are formed during the course of (+)-sesamin biosynthesis can be found in later phases of the seed development but not in leaves or early stages of seed development indicating a narrow window of corresponding biosynthetic activities |CITS: [16785429]|. The enzymatic activity successively converting (+)-pinoresinol to (+)-piperitol and (+)-sesamin has been identified in microsomes of |FRAME:TAX-4182| seeds |CITS: [Kato98]| |CITS: [Jiao98]|. The enzyme, a cytochrome P450-dependent monooxygenase (CYP81Q1) catalyzes the methylenedioxy bridge formation on different aromatic rings on both ends of (+)-pinoresinol, hence acting as a bifunctional protein |CITS: [16785429]|. The cloning of the gene and characterization of the enzyme provided insight into the mode of action and dismissed the previously proposed mechanism of two independently acting cytochrome P450&apos;s involved in those steps |CITS: [Jiao98]|. Alternative routes that circumvent (+)-sesamin and form (+)-sesamolin via (+)-sesamolinol have been proposed but remain to be experimentally verified (|CITS: [Lewis99]| |CITS: [Kato98]|).</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="nilo-poyanco" />
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
